00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:31.680
We won't be writing our own now.

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:33.140
Well now you most likely
will end up learning

00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:35.240
some best practices in API design,

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:37.520
we won't be covering that specifically.

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:38.960
There are many great courses available

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:41.010
that show the creation thought process,

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:42.260
and I've linked to some of my faves

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:44.410
in the notes attached to this video.

00:03:44.410 --> 00:03:45.290
In my opinion,

00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:47.190
the best way to start
learning about any acronym

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:49.410
is to focus on each individual letter.

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:52.590
For API, I think we should
start on the last character,

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:54.380
the I, interface.

00:03:54.380 --> 00:03:57.880
Let's talk a bit about interfaces
that you've seen already.

00:03:57.880 --> 00:04:00.030
Interfaces are all around us.

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:01.450
For instance, this radio here,

00:04:01.450 --> 00:04:03.180
it has a familiar interface.

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:06.010
The interface allows me to do
things like change the volume,

00:04:06.010 --> 00:04:07.110
change the station,

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:08.910
I can turn it off, and on.

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:10.296
I literally have no idea

00:04:10.296 --> 00:04:12.450
how anything inside this thing works,

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:14.870
but still the interface
allows me to control

00:04:14.870 --> 00:04:18.000
the way it works through the
options that are exposed.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:19.420
The magic that is happening

00:04:19.420 --> 00:04:21.627
is completely abstracted away from me,

00:04:21.627 --> 00:04:23.010
and I'm still in control

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:25.860
of what has been determined,
that I, a user, can handle.

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:28.160
Interfaces usually evolve over time,

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:29.980
like an alarm clock radio for example.

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:32.350
It has my familiar volume,
and station controls,

00:04:32.350 --> 00:04:35.360
and it also adds some new
features like changing the time,

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:36.650
and setting the alarm.

00:04:36.650 --> 00:04:40.210
I still have no idea how this
works implementation-wise,

00:04:40.210 --> 00:04:41.400
but the interface is something

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:44.810
that I can understand usually,

00:04:44.810 --> 00:04:46.230
and as technology evolves,

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:48.040
and new options come into existence,

00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:50.800
they make their way into the
new versions of the interface.

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:53.130
Still abstracting away things for us,

00:04:53.130 --> 00:04:56.253
but enabling us to be in
control of the new features.

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:00.530
Sometimes these physical
interfaces get so recognizable

00:05:00.530 --> 00:05:03.420
that they even make their way into GUIs.

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:04.253
Did you hear that?

00:05:04.253 --> 00:05:08.710
There it is again, gooey, or
GUI, Graphical User Interface.

00:05:08.710 --> 00:05:11.190
This is my streaming
music app on my phone,

00:05:11.190 --> 00:05:12.650
and there's my familiar play button

00:05:12.650 --> 00:05:15.180
in the User Interface, or UI,

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:18.020
and I know what that play
button does when I press it,

00:05:18.020 --> 00:05:20.650
but I have no idea how it does it,

00:05:20.650 --> 00:05:23.200
and I'm okay with that
level of abstraction.

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:24.380
I depend on it even.

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:26.360
What would I do in public
transit without music?

00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:27.940
Like talk to people?

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:29.183
Hi, how's it going?

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:34.630
Now the developer who wrote
this streaming music application

00:05:34.630 --> 00:05:36.810
of course understands how
the play button works.

00:05:36.810 --> 00:05:38.870
She coded the button so
that when it gets clicked,

00:05:38.870 --> 00:05:40.410
the music starts playing.

00:05:40.410 --> 00:05:41.410
Here's the thing though.

00:05:41.410 --> 00:05:43.150
Our developer is also working with

00:05:43.150 --> 00:05:45.740
quite a few interfaces
that are abstracting away

00:05:45.740 --> 00:05:47.500
some of the details for her.

00:05:47.500 --> 00:05:48.950
For instance, she wrote code

00:05:48.950 --> 00:05:50.900
that happens when the button is clicked.

00:05:50.900 --> 00:05:54.500
She probably did that using an
Event Handler on the button.

00:05:54.500 --> 00:05:55.950
What she didn't have to do however,

00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:58.220
was write the code that
makes the button interact,

00:05:58.220 --> 00:05:59.360
and change with the press.

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:00.530
She didn't have to write the code

00:06:00.530 --> 00:06:02.520
that makes that familiar clicking noise.

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:04.211
It was already there for her.

00:06:04.211 --> 00:06:06.610
Buttons are in fact an interface.

00:06:06.610 --> 00:06:07.950
They provide the developer

00:06:07.950 --> 00:06:09.980
the means of controlling its interactions

00:06:09.980 --> 00:06:11.330
while abstracting away

00:06:11.330 --> 00:06:14.770
how that actually works,
implementation-wise.

00:06:14.770 --> 00:06:16.580
This button is part of the API,

00:06:16.580 --> 00:06:18.600
or Application Programming Interface,

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:21.100
that's available from
the application framework

00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:22.840
that was used to create the app.

00:06:22.840 --> 00:06:24.710
Because most apps need buttons,

00:06:24.710 --> 00:06:27.439
this interface has been
created to help our developer,

00:06:27.439 --> 00:06:30.600
or application programmer to use.

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:32.320
Now by implementing the button interface,

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:34.440
our developer was able
to make her code run

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:36.160
when the button was clicked.

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:38.200
It's worth noting that the code probably

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:40.860
also uses a media player API

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:43.230
that's provided by the
operating system of the device

00:06:43.230 --> 00:06:45.067
like Android, or iOS.

00:06:45.067 --> 00:06:49.570
This media player API abstracts
away needing to understand

00:06:49.570 --> 00:06:52.840
the details of how to send
audio data to the hardware.

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:54.800
The developer knows that
he just want to call

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:57.897
the play method of the media player API,

00:06:57.897 --> 00:06:59.630
and I suppose too,

00:06:59.630 --> 00:07:01.010
the song isn't stored on the device.

00:07:01.010 --> 00:07:02.640
It's being streamed over the network,

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:04.060
so a web based API call

00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:06.920
has been made over the
internet to get the data.

00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:09.045
It's actually APIs all
the way down to the ones,

00:07:09.045 --> 00:07:12.320
and zeros providing
abstractions at many levels.

00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:13.170
Let's take a look at

00:07:13.170 --> 00:07:15.327
what all these interfaces have in common.

00:07:15.327 --> 00:07:18.190
They all define ways for us to interact,

00:07:18.190 --> 00:07:19.910
or communicate with an object,

00:07:19.910 --> 00:07:22.440
whether that object be
physical, or software,

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:24.310
and as a user of the interface,

00:07:24.310 --> 00:07:26.490
we don't need to understand
the implementation.

00:07:26.490 --> 00:07:27.960
We don't need to know how it works.

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:28.900
We just need to know

00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:31.860
what we've been allowed to change, or see.

00:07:31.860 --> 00:07:34.970
Interfaces abstract
away the implementation.

00:07:34.970 --> 00:07:36.010
Now, while the UI,

00:07:36.010 --> 00:07:39.240
or User Interface is made for
the user of the application,

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:42.400
the API is made for the
application programmer to use,

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:45.190
and extend in their applications

00:07:45.190 --> 00:07:48.070
with the I covered in our API,

00:07:48.070 --> 00:07:51.610
let's take a deeper look at
the rest of the API acronym.

00:07:51.610 --> 00:07:53.610
An API is a contract of sorts,

00:07:53.610 --> 00:07:55.469
it defines how it's expected to be used,

00:07:55.469 --> 00:07:59.520
and it defines what you can
expect to receive by using it.

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:01.070
Think of it as a set of tools

00:08:01.070 --> 00:08:03.830
designed for software
developers like yourself.

00:08:03.830 --> 00:08:06.300
An API is created to help
make your life easier

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:09.910
by giving you access to
data, and by providing you

00:08:09.910 --> 00:08:12.590
with an abstraction of the implementation.

00:08:12.590 --> 00:08:13.510
You know, not needing to know

00:08:13.510 --> 00:08:15.800
how it's doing whatever it's doing.

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:19.320
They save you from needing to
create everything yourself.

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:20.153
When designed well,

00:08:20.153 --> 00:08:22.470
they make doing nearly
impossible things happen,

00:08:22.470 --> 00:08:24.150
in just a few lines of code.

00:08:24.150 --> 00:08:25.770
You're probably starting
to see why it's important

00:08:25.770 --> 00:08:27.265
to get familiar with them, right?

00:08:27.265 --> 00:08:29.210
I want to do a quick word of warning here

00:08:29.210 --> 00:08:32.010
about some naming issues that
you'll probably run into.

00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:35.180
These days, The term API
is almost always used

00:08:35.180 --> 00:08:37.790
as a way to explain web-based APIs.

00:08:37.790 --> 00:08:39.560
In fact, it's probably safe to assume

00:08:39.560 --> 00:08:41.520
that when you hear API in the wild,

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:43.540
it's most likely that
people are talking about

00:08:43.540 --> 00:08:45.030
a web based API.

00:08:45.030 --> 00:08:46.630
Now we'll explore those here in a few.

00:08:46.630 --> 00:08:49.940
However, if we focus on all types of APIs

00:08:49.940 --> 00:08:52.090
instead of just the web based subset,

00:08:52.090 --> 00:08:53.450
we'll get a better look into

00:08:53.450 --> 00:08:55.450
why web-based is are so popular,

00:08:55.450 --> 00:08:57.020
so hang tight, we're getting there.

00:08:57.020 --> 00:08:59.350
APIs are everywhere in
your software journey,

00:08:59.350 --> 00:09:01.590
just about everywhere you look.

00:09:01.590 --> 00:09:02.690
Your programming language,

00:09:02.690 --> 00:09:05.770
whichever one it might
be provides many APIs.

00:09:05.770 --> 00:09:08.610
Let's take a look at one
that's common in all of them.

00:09:08.610 --> 00:09:11.130
They all provide some way
of working with strings,

00:09:11.130 --> 00:09:13.100
you know, like a series of characters.

00:09:13.100 --> 00:09:14.700
You want to make your textile upper case?

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:17.710
Just call the API method, and voila,

00:09:17.710 --> 00:09:19.670
you could probably create
that uppercase string

00:09:19.670 --> 00:09:21.930
all by yourself by doing some sort of

00:09:21.930 --> 00:09:24.110
low level bit math on each character,

00:09:24.110 --> 00:09:25.476
but you don't need to,

00:09:25.476 --> 00:09:27.410
because your programming language

00:09:27.410 --> 00:09:28.820
has abstracted that away for you,

00:09:28.820 --> 00:09:31.760
and provided a handy string API.

00:09:31.760 --> 00:09:33.900
It exists because this is
a common enough problem

00:09:33.900 --> 00:09:35.150
that developers run into,

00:09:35.150 --> 00:09:37.370
because these programming
language specifications

00:09:37.370 --> 00:09:38.710
are just interfaces,

00:09:38.710 --> 00:09:40.020
different operating systems

00:09:40.020 --> 00:09:42.130
can supply their own implementations

00:09:42.130 --> 00:09:44.770
so that the code works
properly in their environment.

00:09:44.770 --> 00:09:47.270
I think an easy way of
picturing this is to think about

00:09:47.270 --> 00:09:49.520
how different operating
systems like Windows,

00:09:49.520 --> 00:09:51.673
or Mac OS maintain their files.

00:09:51.673 --> 00:09:53.310
They're totally different,

00:09:53.310 --> 00:09:54.650
yet your programming language

00:09:54.650 --> 00:09:58.240
provides a single API for
working with the file system.

00:09:58.240 --> 00:09:59.620
You can write one script,

00:09:59.620 --> 00:10:02.192
and it works in either Mac, or Windows.

00:10:02.192 --> 00:10:03.730
It's the implementation

00:10:03.730 --> 00:10:05.870
that takes care of all
the details for you.

00:10:05.870 --> 00:10:07.050
Pretty cool, right?

00:10:07.050 --> 00:10:09.080
Definitely something to be thankful for.

00:10:09.080 --> 00:10:11.980
It makes our life so much
easier not having to worry about

00:10:11.980 --> 00:10:13.900
what operating system is being used.

00:10:13.900 --> 00:10:15.370
The importance of an API

00:10:15.370 --> 00:10:17.440
to a language cannot be understated.

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:19.340
There's been some fascinating legal cases

00:10:19.340 --> 00:10:21.535
where it's been questioned
if the language API itself,

00:10:21.535 --> 00:10:24.651
again, not the implementation
can be copyrighted.

00:10:24.651 --> 00:10:26.650
Check out the notes for more on that.

00:10:26.650 --> 00:10:29.350
Along similar lines, there
are different web browsers,

00:10:29.350 --> 00:10:31.690
you know like Google
Chrome, Microsoft Edge,

00:10:31.690 --> 00:10:33.900
Safari, Firefox, and Brave.

00:10:33.900 --> 00:10:35.950
Now you might not have
pondered this before,

00:10:35.950 --> 00:10:38.850
but isn't it amazing that you
can write code for the web,

00:10:38.850 --> 00:10:40.900
and it will work in any of them?

00:10:40.900 --> 00:10:43.700
Now this works because
there's a set of web APIs

00:10:43.700 --> 00:10:46.030
that web browsers all must implement

00:10:46.030 --> 00:10:48.580
in order to make that
magic happen for you.

00:10:48.580 --> 00:10:51.400
We're standing on the
shoulders of giants folks.

00:10:51.400 --> 00:10:53.780
We really should thank
those giants more often.

00:10:53.780 --> 00:10:54.613
There are authors,

00:10:54.613 --> 00:10:57.130
and maintainers of libraries
that package up code,

00:10:57.130 --> 00:10:58.410
and solve common problems

00:10:58.410 --> 00:11:00.060
so that you don't need to do it yourself.

00:11:00.060 --> 00:11:01.235
Like for instance,

00:11:01.235 --> 00:11:02.900
do you know how to programmatically

00:11:02.900 --> 00:11:04.519
make a photo black and white?

00:11:04.519 --> 00:11:07.800
Mean neither, but I can download
an important image package

00:11:07.800 --> 00:11:09.930
into my application, and suddenly I can.

00:11:09.930 --> 00:11:11.895
I just call a method, and boom,

00:11:11.895 --> 00:11:14.090
I wish real life skills worked this way.

00:11:14.090 --> 00:11:16.017
I'd import dancing so it
could be more fun at weddings,

00:11:16.017 --> 00:11:17.333
cha cha cha.

00:11:17.333 --> 00:11:19.560
Frameworks provide an API

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:20.810
that allow you to extend

00:11:20.810 --> 00:11:22.840
what has been provided
to make it your own.

00:11:22.840 --> 00:11:23.830
Like for example,

00:11:23.830 --> 00:11:26.250
most web frameworks provide
you with the ability

00:11:26.250 --> 00:11:28.490
to add a new route to
your web application.

00:11:28.490 --> 00:11:30.140
It allows you to write out dynamic data

00:11:30.140 --> 00:11:31.930
to a template that you specify,

00:11:31.930 --> 00:11:35.070
and you do all of this with
just a handful of lines of code.

00:11:35.070 --> 00:11:37.200
You don't need to understand
how it's doing things,

00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:39.690
but you do need to know how
to write an implementation

00:11:39.690 --> 00:11:41.560
to what the framework is expecting.

00:11:41.560 --> 00:11:43.510
In this case, you're using the API,

00:11:43.510 --> 00:11:45.960
the contract to write
a valid implementation

00:11:45.960 --> 00:11:47.313
that meets the requirements.

00:11:47.313 --> 00:11:49.770
All of these APIs that
we just talked about,

00:11:49.770 --> 00:11:51.430
they're local to our code base.

00:11:51.430 --> 00:11:52.820
They exist within our code.

00:11:52.820 --> 00:11:54.736
We use them to avoid recreating the wheel.

00:11:54.736 --> 00:11:58.096
That is to say they assist
us in accomplishing tasks

00:11:58.096 --> 00:11:59.840
that we need to perform

00:11:59.840 --> 00:12:02.170
by abstracting away a ton of work for us.

00:12:02.170 --> 00:12:04.650
The stuff that we developers
shouldn't be worrying about.

00:12:04.650 --> 00:12:07.380
We should be focusing on the
business problem at hand,

00:12:07.380 --> 00:12:10.220
and that means a big part
of your job is figuring out

00:12:10.220 --> 00:12:12.795
how to leverage these
APIs to your advantage.

00:12:12.795 --> 00:12:16.650
Now I think it's an excellent
time to take a quick break.

00:12:16.650 --> 00:12:18.570
I just gave you a lot to think about,

00:12:18.570 --> 00:12:19.957
and I'd like you to take
a minute to breathe.

00:12:19.957 --> 00:12:23.380
Why don't you jot down some
APIs that you've used today,

00:12:23.380 --> 00:12:24.440
even if you weren't coding.

00:12:24.440 --> 00:12:26.540
I know that you've seen some in action.

00:12:26.540 --> 00:12:28.430
If you're having a hard
time thinking of some,

00:12:28.430 --> 00:12:29.530
I'll give you a hint.

00:12:29.530 --> 00:12:32.816
You're watching a video right
now inside of a web browser.

00:12:32.816 --> 00:12:34.030
When we return,

00:12:34.030 --> 00:12:36.440
we'll explore the concept of remote APIs,

00:12:36.440 --> 00:12:38.710
ones that aren't executing
locally in our code base,

00:12:38.710 --> 00:12:41.491
and why that's such a powerful concept.

00:12:41.491 --> 00:12:45.074
(calming electronic music)

00:12:56.450 --> 00:12:58.057
It took me a while to
find this remote control.

00:12:58.057 --> 00:12:59.250
It was in the couch.

00:12:59.250 --> 00:13:00.680
It's always in the couch.

00:13:00.680 --> 00:13:03.700
Anywho, this remote has
an interface on it, right?

00:13:03.700 --> 00:13:04.800
There's the power button,

00:13:04.800 --> 00:13:06.130
and there's a channel changer,

00:13:06.130 --> 00:13:07.180
and when I press the volume button,

00:13:07.180 --> 00:13:09.110
it sends my request to my device,

00:13:09.110 --> 00:13:12.160
and the volume changes as
if I turn the volume up

00:13:12.160 --> 00:13:14.140
using the interface on the device itself,

00:13:14.140 --> 00:13:15.730
and we know why that's important.

00:13:15.730 --> 00:13:17.595
I want to remotely control my device.

00:13:17.595 --> 00:13:19.740
I don't want to get up from the couch.

00:13:19.740 --> 00:13:21.090
There are of course more,

00:13:21.090 --> 00:13:23.030
or less lazy reasons as to why you might

00:13:23.030 --> 00:13:24.980
want to remotely control objects.

00:13:24.980 --> 00:13:28.080
This cute little robot, for
instance, has a remote API.

00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:30.200
You can send commands
to it, and it responds.

00:13:30.200 --> 00:13:32.630
It even sends back sensor information.

00:13:32.630 --> 00:13:35.300
These traffic signs can
change based on an API call.

00:13:35.300 --> 00:13:36.163
This drone here,

00:13:36.163 --> 00:13:39.629
it's actually remote controlled
by an app on my phone.

00:13:39.629 --> 00:13:40.483
See?

00:13:41.530 --> 00:13:42.960
It's not just physical objects

00:13:42.960 --> 00:13:44.810
that benefit from remote APIs.

00:13:44.810 --> 00:13:47.720
Oftentimes we don't have
space on our local machines

00:13:47.720 --> 00:13:49.310
for all the data that's available.

00:13:49.310 --> 00:13:51.660
Like for example, have you
ever used one of those apps

00:13:51.660 --> 00:13:53.800
that helps you figure out
what song is planning?

00:13:53.800 --> 00:13:55.160
You send up a snippet of a song

00:13:55.160 --> 00:13:57.720
you heard playing at a grocery
store using your microphone,

00:13:57.720 --> 00:13:59.992
and it sends back
exactly what the song is.

00:13:59.992 --> 00:14:01.850
It's usually Phil Collins.

00:14:01.850 --> 00:14:03.070
It turns out both grocery stores,

00:14:03.070 --> 00:14:05.400
and yours truly, loves Phil Collins.

00:14:05.400 --> 00:14:06.960
You don't have room

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:09.200
for all the songs in
the world on your phone,

00:14:09.200 --> 00:14:10.372
and with this remote API,

00:14:10.372 --> 00:14:12.330
you don't need to have all of them.

00:14:12.330 --> 00:14:14.260
It does all the searching, and processing,

00:14:14.260 --> 00:14:16.570
and it's surprisingly quick.

00:14:16.570 --> 00:14:18.570
How can it possibly do that?

00:14:18.570 --> 00:14:20.850
I don't know, but I love the interface.

00:14:20.850 --> 00:14:22.047
It's so fast.

00:14:22.047 --> 00:14:26.040
There's another benefit of
remote APIs, computational power.

00:14:26.040 --> 00:14:27.900
Because an API removes the limitations

00:14:27.900 --> 00:14:29.010
of your local machine,

00:14:29.010 --> 00:14:32.530
you can gain access to seemingly
infinite amounts of power.

00:14:32.530 --> 00:14:35.784
A good example of that is the
AR app of Google Translate.

00:14:35.784 --> 00:14:37.397
You can use the camera on your phone,

00:14:37.397 --> 00:14:40.180
and in near real time,
it's able to translate

00:14:40.180 --> 00:14:43.820
directly to your screen,
like, instantaneously.

00:14:43.820 --> 00:14:45.440
It's using a ton of processing power

00:14:45.440 --> 00:14:46.660
to make this magic happen,

00:14:46.660 --> 00:14:49.930
and that processing is
happening elsewhere.

00:14:49.930 --> 00:14:51.130
(speaking foreign language)

00:14:51.130 --> 00:14:53.070
It took quite a bit of experimentation,

00:14:53.070 --> 00:14:54.330
and some evolution of ideas,

00:14:54.330 --> 00:14:56.290
but over time we finally turned

00:14:56.290 --> 00:14:59.230
this remote API dream into a reality.

00:14:59.230 --> 00:15:01.480
Now, I'm gonna drop some
history in the teacher's notes,

00:15:01.480 --> 00:15:03.830
but I just want you to
know that the struggle

00:15:03.830 --> 00:15:07.960
to achieve this concept of
remote APIs was very real,

00:15:07.960 --> 00:15:09.030
but most of the problem was that

00:15:09.030 --> 00:15:11.140
there wasn't a standard
that everyone loved.

00:15:11.140 --> 00:15:12.860
Things were pretty clunky for awhile

00:15:12.860 --> 00:15:16.690
as the ideas were sussed
out, but then it happened.

00:15:16.690 --> 00:15:19.180
A new architectural style
appeared that was clear,

00:15:19.180 --> 00:15:20.820
and allowed us developers to interact with

00:15:20.820 --> 00:15:23.270
specific resources over the web,

00:15:23.270 --> 00:15:26.270
and it quite literally changed everything.

00:15:26.270 --> 00:15:28.400
This style rapidly emerged as a winner,

00:15:28.400 --> 00:15:30.610
and just about everyone began offering,

00:15:30.610 --> 00:15:33.680
and consuming APIs over the internet,

00:15:33.680 --> 00:15:35.440
and what I'm talking about here is REST,

00:15:35.440 --> 00:15:37.630
or Representational State Transfer.

00:15:37.630 --> 00:15:39.000
It was the approach that we needed,

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:41.110
and honestly it's the abstraction

00:15:41.110 --> 00:15:43.100
that we developers deserved.

00:15:43.100 --> 00:15:44.450
Like we talked about already,

00:15:44.450 --> 00:15:47.200
the popularity of REST spreads so rapidly.

00:15:47.200 --> 00:15:50.490
It nearly completely
overtook the term API.

00:15:50.490 --> 00:15:53.440
It's like how we use the
word Xerox to mean photocopy,

00:15:53.440 --> 00:15:56.460
even though Xerox is just
a brand of photocopier.

00:15:56.460 --> 00:15:59.560
Xerox does other stuff too, I think.

00:15:59.560 --> 00:16:01.640
That's how pervasive REST is.

00:16:01.640 --> 00:16:05.290
REST is for sure not the end
all be all of remote API's.

00:16:05.290 --> 00:16:06.700
There are definitely some limitations,

00:16:06.700 --> 00:16:08.160
which we'll discuss here in a bit.

00:16:08.160 --> 00:16:09.590
Technology is ever evolving,

00:16:09.590 --> 00:16:12.070
and of course there are movements
that are still advancing

00:16:12.070 --> 00:16:14.600
the concept of the best remote APIs,

00:16:14.600 --> 00:16:17.480
some terms you might've
even heard, like GraphQL.

00:16:17.480 --> 00:16:19.370
More on this in the teacher's notes.

00:16:19.370 --> 00:16:20.570
For the rest of this course,

00:16:20.570 --> 00:16:22.940
we're going to be doing
a deep dive into APIs

00:16:22.940 --> 00:16:25.220
that are written using the REST style.

00:16:25.220 --> 00:16:28.020
When APIs embrace the style
and constraints of REST,

00:16:28.020 --> 00:16:30.200
they're said to be RESTful.

00:16:30.200 --> 00:16:31.251
Cute, right?

00:16:31.251 --> 00:16:34.270
If you're feeling like you
need a little rest, go ahead,

00:16:34.270 --> 00:16:36.330
and pause me before we
do a deeper dive into

00:16:36.330 --> 00:16:38.320
what it means to be RESTful.

00:16:38.320 --> 00:16:40.230
Sorry, that was a bad, and obvious joke,

00:16:40.230 --> 00:16:41.547
and as a developer you
kind of have to make it,

00:16:41.547 --> 00:16:43.600
you don't really have an option,

00:16:43.600 --> 00:16:46.200
and because REST sits on
top of web technologies,

00:16:46.200 --> 00:16:48.690
we should probably do
a whirlwind refresher

00:16:48.690 --> 00:16:50.163
of how the web works.

00:16:50.163 --> 00:16:53.746
(calming electronic music)

00:17:05.550 --> 00:17:07.210
It might've been a while
since you thought about

00:17:07.210 --> 00:17:08.510
how the web works.

00:17:08.510 --> 00:17:09.880
There are a smattering of terms

00:17:09.880 --> 00:17:12.360
that I'd like to reload
into your working memory

00:17:12.360 --> 00:17:15.290
before we talk more
about how REST uses them.

00:17:15.290 --> 00:17:17.060
So let's use the web.

00:17:17.060 --> 00:17:18.150
I've got my computer here,

00:17:18.150 --> 00:17:20.320
and on it I have an internet browser.

00:17:20.320 --> 00:17:22.490
This browser is a web client,

00:17:22.490 --> 00:17:25.400
and I'm going to use it
to connect to a server.

00:17:25.400 --> 00:17:26.233
I'm going to do this

00:17:26.233 --> 00:17:28.630
by putting a location in the address bar,

00:17:28.630 --> 00:17:30.450
a universal resource locator,

00:17:30.450 --> 00:17:32.420
or URL to be exact.

00:17:32.420 --> 00:17:36.050
You might also hear this referred
to by a superset term URI,

00:17:36.050 --> 00:17:38.870
or Universal Resource Identifier.

00:17:38.870 --> 00:17:41.500
Now our URL here has a scheme portion,

00:17:41.500 --> 00:17:44.750
and this one happens to be HTTP.

00:17:44.750 --> 00:17:48.000
HTTP stands for Hypertext
Transfer Protocol.

00:17:48.000 --> 00:17:49.430
Let's break that down bit.

00:17:49.430 --> 00:17:51.400
Let's grab that last part, protocol.

00:17:51.400 --> 00:17:54.460
A protocol is also often
likened to a contract.

00:17:54.460 --> 00:17:56.260
It's a little lower level than an API,

00:17:56.260 --> 00:17:58.010
but it's similar in concept.

00:17:58.010 --> 00:18:00.110
You can think of it as
defining the expectations

00:18:00.110 --> 00:18:01.821
of how to communicate.

00:18:01.821 --> 00:18:04.630
An example that I find
really sticks with me

00:18:04.630 --> 00:18:06.030
is if you've ever been to a rally,

00:18:06.030 --> 00:18:07.940
and someone does one of
those chants where they go,

00:18:07.940 --> 00:18:09.300
when I say food,

00:18:09.300 --> 00:18:14.300
you say bar, food bar, food
bar, here, let's do one.

00:18:14.340 --> 00:18:16.800
When I say H-T-T, you say P!

00:18:16.800 --> 00:18:18.913
H-T-T, H-T-T.

00:18:20.200 --> 00:18:22.810
I just realized I might've
made you yell pee out loud.

00:18:22.810 --> 00:18:25.473
I hope you're in a coffee
shop, that'd be awkward.

00:18:26.840 --> 00:18:29.020
Anyways, we, you, and I,

00:18:29.020 --> 00:18:31.240
we just set up a real quick protocol.

00:18:31.240 --> 00:18:34.110
I told you how to respond
to my specific request,

00:18:34.110 --> 00:18:36.350
you would have been breaking
our agreed upon protocol

00:18:36.350 --> 00:18:38.250
if you yelled anything else, but P.

00:18:38.250 --> 00:18:41.090
So the browser creates
an HTTP request for you.

00:18:41.090 --> 00:18:42.310
Along with the URI,

00:18:42.310 --> 00:18:45.970
it also specifies a particular HTTP verb.

00:18:45.970 --> 00:18:48.320
In this case, it's using the GET verb,

00:18:48.320 --> 00:18:49.963
which is used to clarify

00:18:49.963 --> 00:18:52.590
that this request will only receive data.

00:18:52.590 --> 00:18:54.630
That is, it won't be making any changes.

00:18:54.630 --> 00:18:56.010
The server receives the request

00:18:56.010 --> 00:18:58.010
that was transferred from the client.

00:18:58.010 --> 00:18:59.770
There's that second T.

00:18:59.770 --> 00:19:02.087
The server then does the
work that it needs to do,

00:19:02.087 --> 00:19:04.760
and finally it generates
the resulting webpage,

00:19:04.760 --> 00:19:07.440
and sends back a response to the client,

00:19:07.440 --> 00:19:08.960
my browser in this case.

00:19:08.960 --> 00:19:12.370
Now, the most important part
of that response is the body.

00:19:12.370 --> 00:19:14.760
For a webpage that contains the HTML,

00:19:14.760 --> 00:19:17.020
or Hypertext Markup Language,

00:19:17.020 --> 00:19:18.620
which gives us the remaining parts,

00:19:18.620 --> 00:19:20.750
the HT of our acronym.

00:19:20.750 --> 00:19:23.580
Hypertext is text that
can link to other texts.

00:19:23.580 --> 00:19:26.470
In HTML, we perform that
linking with anchor tags.

00:19:26.470 --> 00:19:27.750
So with that response,

00:19:27.750 --> 00:19:30.070
the browser renders the page.

00:19:30.070 --> 00:19:31.650
When one of those links are clicked,

00:19:31.650 --> 00:19:34.997
they kick off a brand
new HTTP GET request,

00:19:34.997 --> 00:19:38.100
and the process repeats
itself over, and over.

00:19:38.100 --> 00:19:41.040
In between those clicks
though the protocol is done.

00:19:41.040 --> 00:19:43.680
We made the request, we got the response.

00:19:43.680 --> 00:19:47.983
HTTP as it was originally
designed is a Stateless protocol.

00:19:47.983 --> 00:19:50.360
Once that request receives a response,

00:19:50.360 --> 00:19:51.510
it's all done.

00:19:51.510 --> 00:19:53.270
If you want to keep some sort of state,

00:19:53.270 --> 00:19:55.210
your client will have
to manage that itself,

00:19:55.210 --> 00:19:57.700
and set it up with each new requests.

00:19:57.700 --> 00:19:59.080
Check the notes for more on this.

00:19:59.080 --> 00:20:00.840
There are more HTTP verbs,

00:20:00.840 --> 00:20:03.760
also known as request
methods, besides GET.

00:20:03.760 --> 00:20:06.390
Most common scenario you see
is when you encounter a form

00:20:06.390 --> 00:20:08.210
that is used to take information from you,

00:20:08.210 --> 00:20:10.110
typically you'll see the verb post

00:20:10.110 --> 00:20:11.630
used to explain that you are submitting,

00:20:11.630 --> 00:20:13.960
or posting data to the server.

00:20:13.960 --> 00:20:17.310
Okay, so our protocol that's
used to transfer this hypertext

00:20:17.310 --> 00:20:19.420
is admittedly a little more complex

00:20:19.420 --> 00:20:20.920
than our rally chant was.

00:20:20.920 --> 00:20:22.820
There's quite a bit of
important information

00:20:22.820 --> 00:20:25.660
that's included in both the
request and the response.

00:20:25.660 --> 00:20:28.000
You've probably seen query
string parameters, and URLs.

00:20:28.000 --> 00:20:29.990
You know, the stuff
after the question mark?

00:20:29.990 --> 00:20:32.990
This is one way to pass
information about the request.

00:20:32.990 --> 00:20:34.480
Another common way this information

00:20:34.480 --> 00:20:36.450
is passed around is through specific key,

00:20:36.450 --> 00:20:38.560
and value pairs, called headers.

00:20:38.560 --> 00:20:41.480
Both requests, and responses have headers,

00:20:41.480 --> 00:20:43.490
and they're used to
help further communicate

00:20:43.490 --> 00:20:46.450
what is wanted as well as
what has been sent back.

00:20:46.450 --> 00:20:48.660
Now, some of the popular request headers

00:20:48.660 --> 00:20:50.410
allow you to specify information

00:20:50.410 --> 00:20:53.320
about the content that you'd
expect back like the language,

00:20:53.320 --> 00:20:54.680
or the type of content.

00:20:54.680 --> 00:20:56.540
You can also state you only want a page

00:20:56.540 --> 00:20:59.120
if it has changed since a certain time.

00:20:59.120 --> 00:21:00.820
This technique allows for caching

00:21:00.820 --> 00:21:03.100
which is a powerful concept.

00:21:03.100 --> 00:21:04.100
Why get the whole page

00:21:04.100 --> 00:21:06.110
if you already have it
in your local cache?

00:21:06.110 --> 00:21:07.940
You can embed authentication information

00:21:07.940 --> 00:21:09.800
in each request using a header.

00:21:09.800 --> 00:21:11.140
This way you are essentially

00:21:11.140 --> 00:21:13.614
keeping your client
logged in across requests.

00:21:13.614 --> 00:21:16.240
The response also contains headers.

00:21:16.240 --> 00:21:18.910
A super important one is the status code.

00:21:18.910 --> 00:21:21.230
This lets you know what
happened on the server side,

00:21:21.230 --> 00:21:23.380
and it can convey things
like missing documents.

00:21:23.380 --> 00:21:25.630
I'm sure you've seen the old 404 error.

00:21:25.630 --> 00:21:26.760
The page has been moved,

00:21:26.760 --> 00:21:29.810
or hopefully everything is A, 200 OK.

00:21:29.810 --> 00:21:31.870
It can also explain the content type,

00:21:31.870 --> 00:21:33.270
how large the data return is,

00:21:33.270 --> 00:21:35.104
and many more things.

00:21:35.104 --> 00:21:36.488
Okay, got it?

00:21:36.488 --> 00:21:39.130
Feeling good about that
little whirlwind tour?

00:21:39.130 --> 00:21:41.170
Remember, feel free to
make me repeat myself

00:21:41.170 --> 00:21:42.350
if that was way too much.

00:21:42.350 --> 00:21:43.400
I totally get it.

00:21:43.400 --> 00:21:45.370
That was a lot of information.

00:21:45.370 --> 00:21:46.270
It's important to have

00:21:46.270 --> 00:21:47.700
a basic understanding of these things,

00:21:47.700 --> 00:21:49.440
because REST sits on top of them.

00:21:49.440 --> 00:21:51.040
It uses the concepts that were introduced

00:21:51.040 --> 00:21:52.970
with the web to expose resources

00:21:52.970 --> 00:21:55.210
that can be accessed, and manipulated.

00:21:55.210 --> 00:21:56.260
Let's take a look at how

00:21:56.260 --> 00:21:58.380
the RESTful approach takes
advantage of the web,

00:21:58.380 --> 00:22:01.050
and it's tooling for
your benefit, and profit.

00:22:01.050 --> 00:22:03.690
REST stands for
Representational State Transfer.

00:22:03.690 --> 00:22:06.460
APIs that meet the REST
architectural style constraints

00:22:06.460 --> 00:22:08.400
are considered to be RESTful.

00:22:08.400 --> 00:22:10.430
A typical way to teach about RESTful API's

00:22:10.430 --> 00:22:13.443
is to cover the constraints
that define it all up front.

00:22:13.443 --> 00:22:16.220
I don't know about you,
but personally I find

00:22:16.220 --> 00:22:18.840
approaching learning that
way pretty overwhelming,

00:22:18.840 --> 00:22:19.900
especially from the beginning.

00:22:19.900 --> 00:22:22.449
I mean we haven't even
looked at an API yet,

00:22:22.449 --> 00:22:23.770
and on top of that,

00:22:23.770 --> 00:22:24.900
most of the REST APIs

00:22:24.900 --> 00:22:27.650
that you use will meet
all of these constraints.

00:22:27.650 --> 00:22:29.106
So here's the thing,

00:22:29.106 --> 00:22:32.640
I promise that we'll go
over all the constraints,

00:22:32.640 --> 00:22:33.930
but let's do it on our own time.

00:22:33.930 --> 00:22:36.650
Like don't learn them
all, right this instant.

00:22:36.650 --> 00:22:38.540
We can even make it a fun journey

00:22:38.540 --> 00:22:41.460
like a RESTful constraint scavenger hunt,

00:22:41.460 --> 00:22:42.640
complete with prizes.

00:22:42.640 --> 00:22:44.630
Here are the guiding
architectural constraints

00:22:44.630 --> 00:22:47.640
required for an API to
be considered RESTful.

00:22:47.640 --> 00:22:49.170
We'll check them off when we discuss them,

00:22:49.170 --> 00:22:52.090
and then we'll loop back once
more at the end, and review.

00:22:52.090 --> 00:22:53.310
Got to collect them all.

00:22:53.310 --> 00:22:54.770
Sound fun?

00:22:54.770 --> 00:22:55.640
Good.

00:22:55.640 --> 00:22:57.470
Let's get started talking about

00:22:57.470 --> 00:23:00.160
how REST APIs sit on
top of web technology,

00:23:00.160 --> 00:23:01.490
like a little hat.

00:23:01.490 --> 00:23:02.670
Much like the web,

00:23:02.670 --> 00:23:05.270
the client makes a request to a server.

00:23:05.270 --> 00:23:06.410
The client in this case

00:23:06.410 --> 00:23:08.530
is typically going to be your program,

00:23:08.530 --> 00:23:10.687
and you'll probably be
using some sort of library

00:23:10.687 --> 00:23:13.270
to create the request to the server.

00:23:13.270 --> 00:23:16.050
The protocol used is, you guessed it,

00:23:16.050 --> 00:23:18.490
HTTP, and it's Stateless.

00:23:18.490 --> 00:23:19.560
The server won't remember

00:23:19.560 --> 00:23:21.730
anything about the particular client.

00:23:21.730 --> 00:23:24.690
If you want to maintain state
like your login details,

00:23:24.690 --> 00:23:27.050
you must send it with
each, and every request,

00:23:27.050 --> 00:23:28.417
and you'll do this using headers,

00:23:28.417 --> 00:23:29.540
and just like that,

00:23:29.540 --> 00:23:32.140
we've talked about two
of our REST constraints,

00:23:32.140 --> 00:23:34.820
Client-Server Architecture, and Stateless.

00:23:34.820 --> 00:23:36.240
You got two knocked off already.

00:23:36.240 --> 00:23:38.310
You're on a roll now.

00:23:38.310 --> 00:23:39.790
We should probably talk about

00:23:39.790 --> 00:23:43.058
what that request was requesting, right?

00:23:43.058 --> 00:23:47.370
Well, it most certainly was
requesting about a resource.

00:23:47.370 --> 00:23:48.750
A resource is a little abstract,

00:23:48.750 --> 00:23:50.030
and that's intentional.

00:23:50.030 --> 00:23:52.363
It's the R in the URL, or URI,

00:23:53.220 --> 00:23:56.023
we use the term resource
to reference an object,

00:23:56.023 --> 00:23:59.522
which also happens to be
pretty abstract, right?

00:23:59.522 --> 00:24:01.620
This is because we can use resources

00:24:01.620 --> 00:24:04.530
to describe just about
anything that we build.

00:24:04.530 --> 00:24:07.920
For instance, let's imagine
an ebook store website.

00:24:07.920 --> 00:24:09.860
On the homepage, maybe there's a list,

00:24:09.860 --> 00:24:11.680
or a collection of new books.

00:24:11.680 --> 00:24:14.420
Each of these books is a resource,

00:24:14.420 --> 00:24:16.230
and we click into that
to take a closer look

00:24:16.230 --> 00:24:17.320
at the book resource,

00:24:17.320 --> 00:24:18.920
and we see a link to the author,

00:24:18.920 --> 00:24:21.027
which is also a resource.

00:24:21.027 --> 00:24:22.790
We click into the Author Resource,

00:24:22.790 --> 00:24:25.250
and we see a collection
of all the other books

00:24:25.250 --> 00:24:26.083
that they've written.

00:24:26.083 --> 00:24:27.920
Maybe we even see a group of fans.

00:24:27.920 --> 00:24:29.490
Again, another collection,

00:24:29.490 --> 00:24:32.640
and each of those fans are a resource.

00:24:32.640 --> 00:24:33.940
Clicking into a fan resource,

00:24:33.940 --> 00:24:34.850
we can see the collection

00:24:34.850 --> 00:24:36.650
of their favorite authors, and books.

00:24:36.650 --> 00:24:39.189
Everything is a resource.

00:24:39.189 --> 00:24:40.950
Let's change gears.

00:24:40.950 --> 00:24:43.690
How about an image sharing
application on our phone?

00:24:43.690 --> 00:24:45.660
So we have user resources.

00:24:45.660 --> 00:24:47.350
Those users post photos,

00:24:47.350 --> 00:24:49.080
which are resources,

00:24:49.080 --> 00:24:50.530
and the photos can be loved.

00:24:50.530 --> 00:24:52.130
So there's like lover resource.

00:24:52.130 --> 00:24:53.946
Maybe there's an album
that could be created,

00:24:53.946 --> 00:24:54.900
and there's an album resource

00:24:54.900 --> 00:24:56.950
that contains photo resources,

00:24:56.950 --> 00:25:00.250
and albums have a collection
of contributor resources.

00:25:00.250 --> 00:25:03.630
Turns out almost everything
we ever want to build

00:25:03.630 --> 00:25:07.107
can be expressed in terms of
resources, and collections.

00:25:07.107 --> 00:25:08.980
Most of what we want our applications

00:25:08.980 --> 00:25:11.530
to be able to do to their
resources can be expressed through

00:25:11.530 --> 00:25:15.760
the unfortunate sounding acronym
CRUD, and that stands for

00:25:15.760 --> 00:25:18.460
Creating, Reading, Updating, and Deleting.

00:25:18.460 --> 00:25:20.220
Now as a user of the ebook site,

00:25:20.220 --> 00:25:21.704
all we did was a bunch of reading,

00:25:21.704 --> 00:25:23.059
no pun intended.

00:25:23.059 --> 00:25:25.470
Actually I did intend that one.

00:25:25.470 --> 00:25:26.470
We were reading, right?

00:25:26.470 --> 00:25:28.670
Just getting the information to display

00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:31.020
like we retrieved the author page.

00:25:31.020 --> 00:25:33.640
We made a read request for that page.

00:25:33.640 --> 00:25:35.640
The web browser used a GET request

00:25:35.640 --> 00:25:37.910
to that specific author page.

00:25:37.910 --> 00:25:40.470
Now the RESTful API
follows the same logic.

00:25:40.470 --> 00:25:43.444
Your program sends a GET request to a URI.

00:25:43.444 --> 00:25:46.260
Then the survey response with data,

00:25:46.260 --> 00:25:48.400
and of course all the HTTP headers

00:25:48.400 --> 00:25:50.310
that you've come to know, and love,

00:25:50.310 --> 00:25:53.490
and the body is typically
represented these days as JSON,

00:25:53.490 --> 00:25:55.360
or JavaScript Object Notation.

00:25:55.360 --> 00:25:58.570
Now, JSON provides a
great way to structure,

00:25:58.570 --> 00:25:59.860
and nest your data.

00:25:59.860 --> 00:26:02.170
Every programming language
that I can think of

00:26:02.170 --> 00:26:04.669
provide some means of
turning a JSON string

00:26:04.669 --> 00:26:07.020
into a native object.

00:26:07.020 --> 00:26:09.590
Content type is typically
something that you can specify

00:26:09.590 --> 00:26:11.930
on your request if JSON isn't your thing.

00:26:11.930 --> 00:26:15.380
Now, if you recall, there are
other HTTP verbs available.

00:26:15.380 --> 00:26:17.590
These verbs are used in REST APIs

00:26:17.590 --> 00:26:19.633
to clearly state the
intention of your request.

00:26:19.633 --> 00:26:21.130
Want to add an author?

00:26:21.130 --> 00:26:22.480
Post to the /authors,

00:26:22.480 --> 00:26:24.160
and send the data that you want to update

00:26:24.160 --> 00:26:25.800
in the body of your request.

00:26:25.800 --> 00:26:27.890
These fields will be
defined for you somewhere.

00:26:27.890 --> 00:26:29.080
Want to remove an author?

00:26:29.080 --> 00:26:31.450
Send a delete to that specific author.

00:26:31.450 --> 00:26:33.840
Updates typically support the patch verb,

00:26:33.840 --> 00:26:36.440
and you'll see we're
able to represent CRUD,

00:26:36.440 --> 00:26:40.320
the Create, Read, Update, and
Delete using HTTP methods.

00:26:40.320 --> 00:26:41.340
Pretty cool, right?

00:26:41.340 --> 00:26:43.120
What this means is that you can interact

00:26:43.120 --> 00:26:44.530
with any application out there

00:26:44.530 --> 00:26:47.010
that's exposed their REST API.

00:26:47.010 --> 00:26:49.210
You could grab data
remotely automate tasks,

00:26:49.210 --> 00:26:51.110
and even smush services together.

00:26:51.110 --> 00:26:53.570
This is called a mashup,
and here's the thing,

00:26:53.570 --> 00:26:55.560
