02:16:12.330 --> 02:16:14.170
or some actual JavaScript code.

02:16:14.170 --> 02:16:15.950
This doesn't make sense
on our application,

02:16:15.950 --> 02:16:17.030
and that's okay.

02:16:17.030 --> 02:16:19.090
I'm gonna drop this one in the no,

02:16:19.090 --> 02:16:20.579
so we're looking good so far,

02:16:20.579 --> 02:16:22.710
but here comes the doozy.

02:16:22.710 --> 02:16:25.887
So remember this one has
four subsections, rut row.

02:16:25.887 --> 02:16:27.760
Currently we're just representing

02:16:27.760 --> 02:16:29.670
all the complements as strings,

02:16:29.670 --> 02:16:31.974
and absolutely no way to identify them.

02:16:31.974 --> 02:16:34.370
So we fail this sub requirement,

02:16:34.370 --> 02:16:37.070
and therefore this thing is a no,

02:16:37.070 --> 02:16:40.800
but let's keep going
through the rest of these.

02:16:40.800 --> 02:16:41.970
Pun intended, sorry.

02:16:41.970 --> 02:16:43.590
We definitely do not allow

02:16:43.590 --> 02:16:45.260
any sort of manipulation of compliments.

02:16:45.260 --> 02:16:47.031
So this is another fail.

02:16:47.031 --> 02:16:48.890
We haven't gone over this yet,

02:16:48.890 --> 02:16:51.960
but by using headers we
can tell that this is JSON.

02:16:51.960 --> 02:16:53.520
The message sent down has a type,

02:16:53.520 --> 02:16:56.530
and it is clear that it should
be JSON decoded to be used,

02:16:56.530 --> 02:16:58.793
and last, but certainly not least,

02:16:58.793 --> 02:17:03.793
Hateos, or Hate OS is an
often forgotten about part

02:17:05.430 --> 02:17:08.490
of the RESTful constraint,
and very hard to say.

02:17:08.490 --> 02:17:10.980
The idea here is that
there are links provided

02:17:10.980 --> 02:17:13.430
to show off what more
you could do with this,

02:17:13.430 --> 02:17:15.470
and where you could
find related resources.

02:17:15.470 --> 02:17:17.665
It provides URIs, or links.

02:17:17.665 --> 02:17:20.920
We did see this in both the
Spotify, and Twilio ones.

02:17:20.920 --> 02:17:22.970
However, ours doesn't
need to provide links

02:17:22.970 --> 02:17:24.180
to other resources.

02:17:24.180 --> 02:17:26.230
So we don't have a RESTful API,

02:17:26.230 --> 02:17:27.130
and that's okay.

02:17:27.130 --> 02:17:28.180
It works as is.

02:17:28.180 --> 02:17:30.270
It does look like we could
get there pretty quickly

02:17:30.270 --> 02:17:31.420
if we wanted to.

02:17:31.420 --> 02:17:33.760
One thing I want you to
gain from that exercise

02:17:33.760 --> 02:17:36.340
is that you now have the
ability to identify whether,

02:17:36.340 --> 02:17:37.940
or not an API is RESTful,

02:17:37.940 --> 02:17:39.570
and I hope that felt pretty good.

02:17:39.570 --> 02:17:41.970
We've now completed that scavenger hunt.

02:17:41.970 --> 02:17:43.320
We revealed all the constraints,

02:17:43.320 --> 02:17:45.370
and I hope that most of
those are pretty clear.

02:17:45.370 --> 02:17:47.069
Please don't feel like you
need to memorize those.

02:17:47.069 --> 02:17:48.860
You can find these same constraints

02:17:48.860 --> 02:17:50.630
littered all over the internet

02:17:50.630 --> 02:17:53.300
as a prize for finishing the
constraint scavenger hunt,

02:17:53.300 --> 02:17:54.800
I've dropped links to my favorite

02:17:54.800 --> 02:17:57.450
REST API constraint
documentation for you to lean on.

02:17:57.450 --> 02:18:00.000
I've also included links to
popular REST API frameworks

02:18:00.000 --> 02:18:02.280
that will help you design RESTful APIs.

02:18:02.280 --> 02:18:04.310
Make sure to check the notes.

02:18:04.310 --> 02:18:05.370
You did it.

02:18:05.370 --> 02:18:08.660
You just completed a whirlwind
introduction to APIs,

02:18:08.660 --> 02:18:10.960
and I hope you see how much power

02:18:10.960 --> 02:18:13.590
they can add to your programming journey

02:18:13.590 --> 02:18:15.380
by looking at interfaces in general.

02:18:15.380 --> 02:18:17.400
I hope you're more
comfortable with the idea

02:18:17.400 --> 02:18:20.730
of not fully understanding
exactly how something is working,

02:18:20.730 --> 02:18:22.430
but still leaning on that abstraction

02:18:22.430 --> 02:18:24.100
to build your tools, and applications.

02:18:24.100 --> 02:18:26.647
We do this all the time with
physical object interfaces,

02:18:26.647 --> 02:18:27.620
and APIs,

02:18:27.620 --> 02:18:29.710
or Application Programming Interfaces

02:18:29.710 --> 02:18:31.680
aren't all that different.

02:18:31.680 --> 02:18:33.250
I hope you enjoyed taking this course.

02:18:33.250 --> 02:18:35.560
I know I had a ton of fun making it.

02:18:35.560 --> 02:18:36.520
If you think someone you know

02:18:36.520 --> 02:18:39.170
might benefit from this
course, share it with them.

02:18:39.170 --> 02:18:40.410
I'd love to hear your feelings,

02:18:40.410 --> 02:18:41.350
your thoughts, and your dreams.

02:18:41.350 --> 02:18:43.420
So I threw together a little something

02:18:43.420 --> 02:18:46.171
using a smattering of API's
to capture your feedback.

02:18:46.171 --> 02:18:47.760
If you've got the time,

02:18:47.760 --> 02:18:50.510
I'd love to have you answer
a quick text based survey.

02:18:50.510 --> 02:18:55.316
Text feedback to 1-503-461-5537.

02:18:55.316 --> 02:18:56.514
Actually, you know what?

02:18:56.514 --> 02:18:58.070
If you want to, you can also just call,

02:18:58.070 --> 02:18:59.360
and leave me a message too.

02:18:59.360 --> 02:19:01.470
I used an API for that too.

02:19:01.470 --> 02:19:04.040
Please, please, please keep
me posted on your journey,

02:19:04.040 --> 02:19:06.900
and really I can't wait
to see what you build.

02:19:06.900 --> 02:19:08.485
Thanks for hanging out.

